From: "James A. Donald" <jamesdon@infoserv.com>
Subject: operationalism and science

> James Donald:
> > Science assumes that the entities it deals with are real,
> > not merely an arbitrary method of predicting experiments.
> 

In <9310032018.AA22204@netcom4.netcom.com>, dasher@netcom.com (D. Anton Sherwood)  wrote:
> 
>> Science seeks the most accurate predictors; surely that precludes the
> adjective "arbitrary", unless I misunderstand what you mean by it.

This relates to the (false) post structuralist claim that
science is arbitrary, merely another form of religious
belief.  I do not wish to explain post structuralism in
order to refute them.  I had enough grief explaining
operationalism, and was repeatedly and outrageously flamed
for my troubles.

Suppose we attempted to describe the visual world in terms
of the two dimensional images on our retinas, instead of
the three dimensional objects in the external world, which
cannot be directly sensed.

In some cases such an extreme operationalist description
would be quite straight forward.  In other cases impossibly
and vastly complex.

In principle it (though not in practice) it would be
possible to make such a description quite "accurate" in the
sense of "precise".

But such a description would be fundamentally inaccurate
in that it would be false.

Three dimensionally is not just a good trick for making
accurate predictions about two dimensional images.  Things
really are three dimensional.

Further if we were to assume that describing two
dimensional images as if they were really three dimensional
was merely a trick for making accurate predictions about
two dimensional images, this would lead to radically false
predictions about things as yet unseen.  It would mislead
our intuition about what theories were reasonable and what
were unreasonable.

Thus three dimensionally is not just an arbitrary trick
for making predictions, but a fact about the world.

